CONSTRAINT BASED PROBLEM SOLVING

Q1. OPTIMIZED LINUX FILE SYSTEM LAYOUT FOR A 50 GB SERVER

Problem Analysis: The server has 50 GB storage and must manage system
logs, user files and application data. The logs must not exceed 10 GB
and critical services must continue without interruption.

Proposed Partition Layout: 1. /boot - 1 GB - Boot files and kernel 2.
swap - 4 GB - Virtual memory 3. / - 12 GB - Operating system and system
utilities 4. /var - 12 GB - Logs, cache and variable data 5. /home - 10
GB - User files 6. /opt - 6 GB - Application software/data 7.
Unallocated - 5 GB - Emergency expansion

Total = 50 GB

Log Management: The /var partition should contain /var/log. logrotate
can be used to rotate, compress and remove old logs.

Example: sudo nano /etc/logrotate.d/serverlogs

Example configuration:

/var/log/*.log { daily rotate 7 compress missingok notifempty }

Check log usage: du -sh /var/log

Check disk usage: df -h

Monitoring script:

#!/bin/bash

USAGE=$(df /var | awk ‘NR==2 {print $5}’ | tr -d ‘%’)

if [ “$USAGE” -ge 85 ]; then echo “Warning: /var usage is above 85%” fi

Advantages: 1. Separate /var prevents logs from filling the root
filesystem. 2. /home is isolated from system files. 3. /opt protects
application storage. 4. Log rotation prevents uncontrolled log growth.
5. Unallocated space provides emergency capacity. 6. df and du allow
continuous monitoring. 7. Critical services are less likely to stop
because of disk exhaustion.

Conclusion: The proposed layout provides controlled storage allocation,
log management, monitoring and emergency capacity. Separating /var,
/home and /opt improves system stability, while logrotate keeps log
files within the 10 GB constraint.


Q2. KERNEL-LEVEL OPTIMIZATION FOR A HIGH-CPU LINUX SYSTEM

Problem Analysis: The Linux system has high CPU workload and frequent
context switching. Hardware upgrades are not possible, so kernel
scheduling and process management must be optimized.

1.  Analyze CPU usage:

top

ps -eo pid,ppid,comm,%cpu –sort=-%cpu

vmstat 1

mpstat -P ALL 1

2.  Process Priority: Use nice and renice to control process scheduling
    priority.

sudo renice -10 -p PID

nice -n -10 ./application

3.  Real-Time Scheduling: For real-time processing:

sudo chrt -f 80 ./realtime_app

4.  CPU Affinity: Assign a process to a specific CPU:

taskset -c 2 ./application

For an existing process:

taskset -cp 2 PID

5.  Kernel Parameters:

sysctl -a

sudo sysctl -w parameter=value

Persistent parameters can be stored in /etc/sysctl.conf.

6.  Kernel Modules:

lsmod

sudo modprobe -r module_name

sudo modprobe module_name

7.  Reduce Context Switching:

-   Improve CPU affinity.
-   Avoid unnecessary threads.
-   Use suitable scheduling policies.
-   Reduce excessive process creation.
-   Assign appropriate priorities.

Optimization Flow: Monitor CPU usage -> Identify high CPU processes ->
Measure context switching -> Tune process priority -> Set CPU affinity
-> Use real-time scheduling where required -> Remove unnecessary modules
-> Monitor again

Conclusion: Kernel scheduler tuning, process priority, CPU affinity,
real-time scheduling and suitable kernel-module management can reduce
scheduling overhead and improve real-time response without requiring
hardware upgrades.



Q3. PROCESSING 1 MILLION LOG ENTRIES WITHIN 2 MINUTES USING SHELL
SCRIPTING

Problem Analysis: The administrator must process 1 million log entries
per day using only shell scripting and complete the task within 2
minutes.

The main requirement is to minimize process creation and unnecessary
disk I/O.

Efficient Approach: Use awk to process the complete file in a single
pass instead of launching commands for every line.

Example:

awk ‘{count[$3]++} END {for (x in count) print x, count[x]}’ access.log

Count error entries:

awk ‘$4 == “ERROR” {count++} END {print “Errors:”, count}’ access.log

Count HTTP status codes:

awk ‘{count[$9]++} END {for (x in count) print x, count[x]}’ access.log

Search for errors:

grep -F “ERROR” access.log

Count errors:

grep -Fc “ERROR” access.log

Sort reduced results:

awk ‘{count[$3]++} END {for (x in count) print count[x],x}’ access.log |
sort -nr

Efficient shell script:

#!/bin/bash

LOGFILE=“access.log”

echo “Total Entries:” wc -l < “$LOGFILE”

echo “Error Entries:” grep -Fc “ERROR” “$LOGFILE”

echo “Status Code Summary:” awk ’{count[$9]++} END {
    for (code in count)
        print code, count[code]
}' "$LOGFILE”

Measure execution time:

time ./logprocess.sh

Why These Commands Are Suitable: 1. awk processes records sequentially
and supports filtering and counting. 2. grep is optimized for text
searching. 3. wc efficiently counts lines. 4. sort should be used only
after reducing the data. 5. Avoid a shell loop that starts a separate
command for every log entry.

Conclusion: Using awk, grep, wc and selective sort minimizes process
creation and unnecessary I/O. A single-pass approach provides the best
chance of meeting the 2-minute constraint. Actual execution time should
be verified using the time command because it depends on hardware,
storage and log complexity.



Q4. MANAGING A FAULTY PROCESS CONSUMING 90% CPU WITHOUT RESTARTING THE
SYSTEM

Problem Analysis: A background process is consuming 90% CPU. The system
must continue operating without rebooting and critical services must
remain unaffected.

1.  Identify the process:

top

ps -eo pid,ppid,comm,%cpu,%mem –sort=-%cpu

2.  Monitor the process:

top -p PID

ps -p PID -o pid,ppid,stat,ni,%cpu,%mem,cmd

3.  Lower its priority:

sudo renice 10 -p PID

4.  Temporarily stop the process:

kill -STOP PID

Resume it:

kill -CONT PID

5.  Terminate the faulty process:

kill PID

If it does not respond:

kill -9 PID

SIGKILL should be used only as a last resort.

6.  If it is a systemd service:

systemctl status service_name

sudo systemctl restart service_name

This restarts only the affected service and does not require a system
reboot.

7.  Verify the process before taking action:

ps -fp PID

8.  Monitor after the action:

top

uptime

Recommended Strategy: Identify high-CPU PID -> Verify process and parent
-> Check whether it is critical -> Lower priority using renice ->
Monitor CPU usage -> STOP if necessary -> Investigate -> CONT or
terminate -> Verify critical services

Conclusion: Tools such as top, ps, renice, kill and systemctl allow
administrators to identify, control and recover from a faulty high-CPU
process without restarting the entire system.



Q5. RESOURCE ALLOCATION FOR 5 VIRTUAL MACHINES WITH 16 GB RAM

Problem Analysis: The physical server has 16 GB RAM. It must host 5 VMs,
each requiring a minimum of 2 GB, while the host operating system
requires 4 GB.

RAM available for VMs:

16 GB - 4 GB = 12 GB

Minimum RAM required by VMs:

5 x 2 GB = 10 GB

Remaining RAM:

12 GB - 10 GB = 2 GB

Equal Allocation: Host OS = 4 GB VM1 = 2.4 GB VM2 = 2.4 GB VM3 = 2.4 GB
VM4 = 2.4 GB VM5 = 2.4 GB

Total:

4 + (5 x 2.4) = 16 GB

Every VM receives more than the required 2 GB minimum.

Dynamic Allocation: If workloads differ, virtualization technologies can
dynamically allocate memory according to demand. Each VM should have a
minimum of 2 GB and the host should retain 4 GB.

Important virtualization concepts:

1.  Memory Overcommit: Virtual memory can be allocated according to
    expected workload, but excessive overcommit should be avoided
    because it may cause swapping.

2.  Memory Ballooning: The hypervisor can reclaim unused memory from one
    VM and provide it to another VM that requires additional memory.

3.  Resource Priorities: High-priority VMs can receive additional
    resources when required.

4.  Monitoring: Host memory can be monitored using:

free -h

top

Recommended Allocation:

Physical RAM = 16 GB Host OS = 4 GB VM1 = 2.4 GB VM2 = 2.4 GB VM3 = 2.4
GB VM4 = 2.4 GB VM5 = 2.4 GB

Conclusion: The 16 GB RAM constraint can support all 5 VMs because 4 GB
is reserved for the host and 12 GB remains for the VMs. Allocating 2.4
GB to each VM satisfies the 2 GB minimum requirement. Dynamic allocation
and memory ballooning can improve resource utilization when VM workloads
differ.
